<http://data.silknow.org/object/6c4233d5-d6cd-3fdb-806b-1e75934b3569/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "A complete length of cloth in woven silk and gold thread, of the type known as 'kincob'. Varanasi, mid 19th century. This cloth was acquired by the East India Company Museum in 1855 probably came from the Paris International Exhibition of that year. The label gives the name of the cloth in contemorary spelling and and carries the name of the craftsman:  KAMKHAB JUNGALAH MINAKAR by BUDHU. 'Jungalah minakar' (also written in Persian script in ink on the same label) means a creeper or 'jungle' design worked in multi-coloured and finely detailed weaving which is likened to enamelling on metal (minakar)."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/6c4233d5-d6cd-3fdb-806b-1e75934b3569> .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/6c4233d5-d6cd-3fdb-806b-1e75934b3569/dimension/2>
